# Copy to `.env` and fill in. `.env` is gitignored; this file is not, so never
# put a real credential here.
#
# `scripts/build-signed.sh` reads `.env` before building. Everything is
# optional: with none of it set you get an ad-hoc signed build, which is right
# for local work and makes macOS warn about an unidentified developer when
# handed to anyone else.
#
# CAREFUL IF COPYING FROM AN ELECTRON FORGE PROJECT. Tauri uses some of the
# same names for different things:
#
#   Forge                                  Tauri
#   APPLE_API_KEY   = path to the .p8      APPLE_API_KEY      = the key ID
#   APPLE_API_KEY_ID = the key ID          APPLE_API_KEY_PATH = path to the .p8
#   MACOS_SIGN_IDENTITY                    APPLE_SIGNING_IDENTITY
#   WINDOWS_CERT_FILE / _PASSWORD          WINDOWS_CERTIFICATE (base64) / _PASSWORD
#
# Copying a Forge .env across verbatim fails in a confusing way: Tauri tries to
# use the file path as a key ID and Apple rejects it.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- macOS ---
# Signing. `security find-identity -v -p codesigning` lists what you have.
# The full string, e.g. "Developer ID Application: Your Name (ABCDE12345)".
APPLE_SIGNING_IDENTITY=

# Notarization credentials — fill in ONE of the two sets below. Tauri prefers
# the API key if both are complete. Signed but un-notarized still warns on
# first launch, so this is not optional if the build is going to other people.
#
# (a) App Store Connect API key (preferred: team-owned, so it survives an
#     Apple ID password or 2FA change). App Store Connect -> Users and Access
#     -> Integrations -> Keys -> generate.
#     APPLE_API_KEY is the key ID, roughly ten characters.
#     APPLE_API_KEY_PATH is the filesystem path to the downloaded .p8.
APPLE_API_KEY=
APPLE_API_ISSUER=
APPLE_API_KEY_PATH=
#
# (b) Apple ID + app-specific password. APPLE_PASSWORD is an app-specific
#     password from appleid.apple.com — NOT your Apple ID password.
#     APPLE_TEAM_ID is ten characters and is now required for this route.
# APPLE_ID=
# APPLE_PASSWORD=
# APPLE_TEAM_ID=

# Notarization is a round trip to Apple that takes minutes, so it is worth
# turning off while iterating on a signed build. Unset (or anything but 0) =
# notarize whenever the credentials above are complete; 0 = sign only.
# Tauri has no such switch of its own; build-signed.sh implements it by
# withholding the credentials.
NOTARIZE=

# -------------------------------------------------------------- Windows ---
# A code-signing certificate, which stops SmartScreen warning. Tauri wants the
# .pfx base64 encoded rather than as a path:
#   base64 -i cert.pfx | pbcopy
#
# No Windows installer can be built on a Mac, so these only matter on a Windows
# machine or in the release workflow, where they come from repository secrets
# rather than from this file.
# WINDOWS_CERTIFICATE=
# WINDOWS_CERTIFICATE_PASSWORD=
